## Step 4: Configure Kafka Settings

Create `appsettings.json` in your test project root. This is the real file used by both test projects in this repo, trimmed to the parts that matter for MessageHook:

```json
{
  "Logging": {
    "LogLevel": {
      "Default": "Information",
      "Microsoft": "Warning",
      "Microsoft.Hosting.Lifetime": "Information"
    }
  },
  "KafkaBrokerConfiguration": {
    "BootstrapServers": [ "localhost:9092" ],
    "Credentials": {
      "SecurityProtocol": "Plaintext",
      "TlsEnabled": false
    }
  }
}
```

For a non-local broker that requires authentication, use Steeltoe placeholders instead of literal values so nothing sensitive is committed:

```json
"KafkaBrokerConfiguration": {
  "BootstrapServers": [ "${KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP}" ],
  "Credentials": {
    "Username":         "${KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME}",
    "Password":         "${KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD}",
    "Mechanism":        "${KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM?plain}",
    "TlsEnabled":       "true",
    "SecurityProtocol": "SaslSsl"
  }
}
```

Then set the matching environment variables:

```bash
export KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP="your-kafka-bootstrap-servers"
export KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME="your-username"
export KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD="your-password"
```

A local broker for development is one `docker compose -f docker-compose.kafka.yml up` away — see the [docker-compose.kafka.yml](docker-compose.kafka.yml) in the repo root.

